        'What to Expect During Your First Week of College' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The first week is mostly setup: finding rooms, learning each instructor’s expectations, fixing schedule or account problems, and beginning routines before assignments accumulate. Feeling excited, lost, lonely, or tired can all be normal. Treat the week as an information-gathering period, not a test of whether you “belong” in college.</p>

<h2>Before the first class</h2>
<p>Check the official schedule on the morning of each class because rooms and instructors can change. Map buildings, transit, parking, and accessible entrances. Download the school’s emergency information and learning-management app if used. Bring a way to take notes, course materials named by the instructor, water, identification, and a charger.</p>

<h2>In every class</h2>
<p>Confirm the syllabus, required materials, attendance rules, assignment locations, exam dates, grading method, communication channel, and office hours. Write deadlines in one calendar. An LMS “to-do” list may omit undated work, so use the syllabus and instructor announcements as well. Ask promptly when instructions conflict.</p>

<h2>Expect schedule decisions</h2>
<p>You may discover a missing prerequisite, wrong placement, long walk between classes, closed lab, or workload mismatch. Check add, drop, waitlist, tuition-refund, and financial-aid deadlines before changing anything. Dropping a course can affect full-time status, aid, housing, athletics, benefits, and immigration status.</p>

<h2>Complete administrative tasks</h2>
<p>Resolve account holds, financial-aid documents, billing, identification cards, health or immunization requirements, payroll paperwork, and required training. Verify where official notices arrive and enable multifactor authentication. Do not ignore a balance because aid is “pending”; ask student accounts and financial aid what remains.</p>

<h2>Learn the support map</h2>
<p>Locate advising, tutoring, library help, the registrar, financial aid, student accounts, health and counseling services, accessibility services, career services, campus safety, dining, and the dean of students. Students with disabilities generally need to request postsecondary accommodations through the designated office rather than assume a high-school plan transfers automatically.</p>

<h2>Begin socially at a manageable pace</h2>
<p>Introduce yourself to one person in each class, attend one welcome event, and learn one shared space. You do not need to join everything or find lifelong friends immediately. Keep sleep, meals, medication, movement, and downtime in the schedule. Avoid pressure to drink, use substances, overspend, share passwords, or participate in degrading “traditions.”</p>

<h2>End-of-week reset</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Review every syllabus and calendar.</li>
  <li>Confirm the final schedule and account status.</li>
  <li>Plan study blocks for the next two weeks.</li>
  <li>Contact one instructor, advisor, or support office about unresolved issues.</li>
  <li>Choose a few activities worth trying rather than committing to all of them.</li>
</ol>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: first-time college student guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: disability transition to college</a></li>
