        'First-Year Student Survival Guide' => <<<'HTML'
<p>First-year success is less about knowing everything and more about noticing problems early, using the right office, and building repeatable routines. College offers more independence than high school, but deadlines and consequences are often less forgiving. Ask before a small uncertainty becomes a missed requirement.</p>

<h2>Use one planning system</h2>
<p>Combine class meetings, assignments, exams, work, commuting, meals, sleep, and administrative deadlines in one calendar. Each week, review the next two weeks and break large assignments into starts, drafts, and final submissions. Schedule study time as an appointment rather than hoping free time appears.</p>

<h2>Learn actively</h2>
<p>Attend, prepare, take notes in your own words, practice retrieval, and begin assignments while questions can still be answered. Use office hours, tutoring, writing support, librarians, supplemental instruction, and study groups. Ask what to improve after the first assessment instead of treating one grade as a verdict.</p>

<h2>Track the official record</h2>
<p>Read the catalog, degree audit, syllabus, and student handbook. Verify registration, placement, prerequisites, and program milestones with an advisor. Know add/drop, withdrawal, refund, satisfactory academic progress, and registration dates. An advisor helps interpret rules, but save approvals and monitor your own record.</p>

<h2>Manage money and aid</h2>
<p>Build a semester budget that includes books, supplies, food, transportation, housing, and emergencies. Confirm aid requirements and borrow only what you need. Federal Work-Study is earned through a job and does not automatically credit your bill. Renew the FAFSA for every year you want aid and meet school and state deadlines.</p>

<h2>Protect health and access</h2>
<p>Make room for sleep, regular food, movement, healthcare, and prescribed treatment. Learn urgent-care and counseling options before a crisis. Contact accessibility services early if a disability creates barriers; postsecondary students generally must request adjustments. For immediate danger call 911 or campus emergency services; in a U.S. mental-health crisis, call or text 988.</p>

<h2>Build connection gradually</h2>
<p>Talk with classmates, attend recurring events, and try organizations related to an interest, identity, service goal, faith, sport, or career. Reliable friendships usually grow from repeated contact. Do not mistake hazing, humiliation, coercion, dangerous consumption, financial pressure, or secrecy for belonging.</p>

<h2>Respond to setbacks</h2>
<p>When something goes wrong, name the issue, find the applicable rule, contact the person with authority, and propose a next step. Keep messages and deadlines. One failed test, conflict, or homesick week does not define the year, but disappearing from class or ignoring notices can narrow options.</p>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: first-time student guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/8-things-federal-work-study/">Federal Student Aid: Federal Work-Study</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://988lifeline.org/">988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: disability access in college</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Services and policies vary; use your institution’s current catalog, calendar, handbook, and support directory.</em></p>
