        'Making Friends in College' => <<<'HTML'
<p>College friendships usually form through repeated, low-pressure contact rather than one perfect welcome-week event. Aim first for familiarity: learn names, show up consistently, and invite people to a specific small activity. A few dependable connections matter more than appearing socially busy.</p>

<h2>Start where repetition already exists</h2>
<p>Arrive a little early to class, sit near familiar people, join a study group, eat in the same place, or attend a recurring club, job, volunteer shift, cultural center, faith group, commuter event, or recreation program. Repeated encounters make conversation and follow-up easier.</p>

<h2>Use simple invitations</h2>
<p>Ask a classmate to compare notes, walk to the next building, attend an event, or study for 45 minutes. Make the time and activity specific and allow an easy no. If someone declines, it may reflect schedule, work, caregiving, commuting, anxiety, or energy—not rejection.</p>

<h2>Move from acquaintance to friendship</h2>
<p>Follow up, remember details, share gradually, and make plans again. Reciprocity matters: both people should be able to initiate, speak, set boundaries, and change plans. Avoid pressuring someone for private information, constant availability, substances, money, rides, romantic attention, or access to a room or account.</p>

<h2>Include different college experiences</h2>
<p>Commuters, international students, transfer students, student parents, older students, students with disabilities, and working students may not be available late at night. Suggest daytime, accessible, low-cost activities and communicate location and duration. Do not assume everyone drinks, has a car, lives nearby, or can pay.</p>

<h2>Handle loneliness honestly</h2>
<p>It can take weeks or months to feel connected. Reduce passive comparison on social media and keep contact with supportive people outside college while building local ties. If loneliness becomes persistent distress, interferes with daily functioning, or creates thoughts of self-harm, contact counseling or healthcare; for a U.S. crisis, call or text 988.</p>

<h2>Stay safe while socializing</h2>
<p>Meet new people in public or familiar settings, control your transportation, protect drinks and belongings, and tell a trusted person where you are when appropriate. Learn campus emergency and confidential-support options. Leave any activity involving coercion, hazing, humiliation, dangerous consumption, or inability to consent.</p>

<h2>A practical weekly goal</h2>
<p>Have three short conversations, attend one recurring activity, send one follow-up invitation, and check in with one existing support person. If a setting repeatedly feels unwelcoming, try another; it is normal to sample several communities before finding a fit.</p>

<hr><h2>Official support references</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: first-time student tips and campus resources</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security resources</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://988lifeline.org/">988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Use local emergency, counseling, accessibility, and student-life resources for individualized support.</em></p>
