        'Commuter Student Survival Guide' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Commuter students need a reliable travel plan and a campus “home base.” The goal is not to copy residential life; it is to make classes, study, meals, support, and connection work within the time and transportation you actually have.</p>

<h2>Plan door to classroom</h2>
<p>Test the trip during class hours and include traffic, transfers, parking search, shuttles, and walking. Save the last transit departure and severe-weather procedures. Leave a realistic buffer during the first weeks. A parking permit may not guarantee a nearby or available space.</p>

<h2>Build a complete commuter kit</h2>
<p>Carry chargers, medication, water, food, weather gear, personal-care items, and course materials for the full day. Identify lockers or secure storage rather than leaving valuables visible in a vehicle. Keep emergency contacts, transit alerts, roadside information, and a backup ride plan.</p>

<h2>Create a campus base</h2>
<p>Locate the commuter lounge, library, quiet study rooms, microwaves, refrigerators, accessible restrooms, charging outlets, fitness facilities, and food open during your schedule. Learn where you can participate in an online class privately between in-person courses.</p>

<h2>Design the academic schedule</h2>
<p>Grouping classes can reduce travel, but an extremely long day can harm attention and sleep. Leave time for office hours, tutoring, advising, and meals. Before taking back-to-back courses, map the buildings. Check add/drop and aid consequences before changing enrollment to solve a commute problem.</p>

<h2>Build connection in daylight hours</h2>
<p>Talk to classmates, attend a recurring lunchtime or afternoon organization, use commuter events, and suggest study sessions immediately before or after class. Tell group members when you must catch transit or leave for work. Ask whether meetings offer hybrid participation, but do not assume organizations must provide it.</p>

<h2>Track the full cost</h2>
<p>Budget fuel, fares, parking, tolls, insurance, repairs, occasional rideshare, and food purchased because you are away all day. Transportation can be part of a school’s cost-of-attendance estimate, but the allowance may not match actual cost. Ask financial aid about documented special circumstances.</p>

<h2>Review after two weeks</h2>
<p>Measure travel time, cost, fatigue, missed connections, and study completed. Adjust departure times, passes, carpool, parking location, or future class selection. If transportation or campus design creates a disability barrier, contact accessibility services and the relevant transit provider about available processes.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/evaluating-financial-aid-offers/">Federal Student Aid: commuting in total college cost</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.transit.dot.gov/regulations-and-guidance/environmental-programs/public-transit-united-states">Federal Transit Administration: public transit</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: postsecondary disability access</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Transportation, parking, attendance, and campus-access policies vary locally.</em></p>
