        'Living on Campus vs. Commuting' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Living on campus buys proximity; commuting preserves an existing home base and may reduce some costs. Neither is automatically cheaper or more social. Compare total cost, time, contracts, support needs, and the routine each option makes possible for an entire academic year.</p>

<h2>Compare equal time periods</h2>
<p>Campus housing and meal plans may cover academic terms, while home or transportation expenses run year-round. For campus living, include housing, required dining, deposits, fees, laundry, travel home, moving, and storage. For commuting, include household contribution, food, transit or vehicle costs, parking, tolls, and extra time.</p>

<h2>Value time explicitly</h2>
<p>Estimate weekly door-to-seat commuting, not mileage alone. Include delays and the trip to work, groceries, healthcare, and evening events. Campus residents may spend less time traveling to class but more time moving out during breaks or traveling home. Decide what you would do with the time saved.</p>

<h2>Review contracts and eligibility</h2>
<p>Some institutions require designated students to live on campus or purchase a meal plan. Review exemption, cancellation, withdrawal, break-housing, guest, and conduct rules before committing. A change in enrollment may affect campus housing, while it normally does not end an off-campus lease or household obligation.</p>

<h2>Consider academics and involvement</h2>
<p>Living nearby can simplify early classes, study groups, office hours, and late events, but it does not guarantee engagement. Commuters can participate by building campus time around classes and choosing recurring activities. Compare access to quiet study, internet, labs, and a reliable place between classes.</p>

<h2>Consider wellbeing and responsibilities</h2>
<p>Home may provide family support, caregiving access, familiar food, or privacy—or it may make studying difficult. Campus living offers independence and peer contact but can bring noise, roommate stress, and less control. Review healthcare, disability access, safety, cultural or religious needs, and pet or family responsibilities.</p>

<h2>Check aid and payment timing</h2>
<p>Housing and food are included in cost-of-attendance methodology, but aid is individualized and may not equal actual expense. Campus charges may appear on the student account; commuting costs are usually paid directly by the student. Compare grants and scholarships before loans and ask when refunds become available.</p>

<h2>Make the decision reversible where possible</h2>
<p>Ask about housing application and cancellation dates, commuter meal plans, parking waitlists, and midyear changes. Keep a backup for transportation, housing closures, family changes, or roommate problems. Choose the option that remains workable during a demanding week, not only orientation.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: college living expenses</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/evaluating-financial-aid-offers/">Federal Student Aid: comparing total costs</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: disability access</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Your housing agreement, residency rule, aid budget, and transportation options control.</em></p>
