        'Dorm Life: What to Expect' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A residence hall combines a bedroom with a shared community and a school housing agreement. Expect less control over noise, bathrooms, guests, temperature, and shared space than in many family homes. Learn the building systems and talk about expectations before small irritations become conflict.</p>

<h2>Read the housing agreement</h2>
<p>Review payment, occupancy dates, breaks, room changes, guests, quiet hours, prohibited items, pets, alcohol, smoking, decorations, inspections, keys, damage, cancellation, and conduct procedures. Campus housing rules can differ from a private lease. Withdrawal from school may affect eligibility but does not guarantee a full housing refund.</p>

<h2>Learn the room and building</h2>
<p>Complete the condition report and save dated photos. Test locks, windows, alarms, outlets, water, furniture, and accessibility features. Locate exits, severe-weather areas, laundry, mail, trash, kitchens, front desk, and emergency equipment. Report defects through the official maintenance system rather than assuming resident staff can repair them.</p>

<h2>Meet the people who support the hall</h2>
<p>Resident assistants or similar student staff can explain community rules and connect residents to resources, but they are not therapists, lawyers, or emergency responders. Learn the professional housing contact, after-hours maintenance, campus safety, health services, accessibility office, and confidential support options.</p>

<h2>Set roommate expectations</h2>
<p>Discuss sleep, study, alarms, temperature, cleaning, food, guests, borrowing, privacy, substances, and communication. Write the agreement requested by housing and revisit it. A roommate does not need to become a close friend; respectful coexistence is a valid goal.</p>

<h2>Protect health, safety, and property</h2>
<p>Lock the room, control keys and access credentials, follow cooking and fire rules, and do not disable alarms. Keep medication and valuables secure. Do not prop exterior doors or admit unknown people. For immediate danger call 911 or campus emergency services and follow alerts and evacuation instructions.</p>

<h2>Handle conflict early</h2>
<p>Describe specific behavior and propose a change at a calm time. Use resident staff mediation or the room-change process if needed. Document serious or repeated concerns. Threats, harassment, discrimination, stalking, violence, or disability-access failures should go to the appropriate safety, conduct, civil-rights, or accessibility channel.</p>

<h2>Plan for breaks and move-out</h2>
<p>Know when halls close, whether you must leave, which services stop, and how to request break housing. At move-out, follow cleaning, inspection, key, forwarding-address, and storage instructions. Photograph the final condition and retain the closing statement or charge appeal deadline.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2025-2026/vol2/ch6-consumer-information-and-school-reporting">Federal Student Aid Handbook: security and fire-safety disclosures</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: disability access</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Your housing agreement, residence-life rules, and emergency instructions control.</em></p>
