        'Living With Roommates' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Successful roommate relationships rely on explicit expectations, not mind-reading or instant friendship. Discuss money, space, schedules, guests, cleaning, and communication early. The goal is a safe and workable home where each person can study, sleep, and use shared areas.</p>

<h2>Understand the housing structure</h2>
<p>In a residence hall, school housing rules and staff processes apply. Off campus, determine whether everyone signed one joint lease or separate leases and whether any person is a guarantor. Under some joint leases, one tenant can be responsible for rent or damage another tenant fails to pay.</p>

<h2>Create a written agreement</h2>
<p>Cover rent and utilities, cleaning, food, shared purchases, guests, quiet hours, temperature, security, borrowing, pets, parking, and communication. Be specific about who does what and when. A roommate agreement helps among roommates but normally does not alter duties owed to housing or the landlord.</p>

<h2>Protect privacy and boundaries</h2>
<p>Do not enter bedrooms, use belongings, photograph, record, share access codes, disclose private information, or invite people into someone’s sleeping space without permission. Ask rather than assume. Roommates may have different cultures, identities, health needs, finances, and schedules without owing a full personal explanation.</p>

<h2>Communicate before resentment builds</h2>
<p>Choose a calm time, name one observable behavior, describe its impact, listen, and agree on a concrete next step. Use “please wash pans the same day” rather than “you are inconsiderate.” Schedule a brief check-in after move-in and whenever work, class, health, or guest patterns change.</p>

<h2>Use help appropriately</h2>
<p>Resident staff, housing personnel, an off-campus housing office, or a mediator may help with ordinary conflict. Accessibility services or the housing provider handles disability-related accommodation requests. Staff cannot guarantee that roommates become friends, and a preferred room change may not be immediately available.</p>

<h2>Respond to safety concerns</h2>
<p>For immediate danger call 911 or campus emergency services. Threats, violence, stalking, harassment, theft, or serious property damage are not ordinary chore disputes. Preserve messages and relevant evidence, follow safety instructions, and use confidential support if desired. Do not retaliate or attempt an unsafe confrontation.</p>

<h2>Plan departures and replacements</h2>
<p>Follow the housing agreement or lease for room changes, subletting, replacement tenants, keys, inspections, and deposits. Moving out does not automatically end payment liability. Get any release or approved change in writing and update the roommate agreement when membership changes.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/tenant-rights">USAGov: leases and state tenant-rights help</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.hud.gov/stat/fheo/rights-obligations">HUD: fair-housing rights and obligations</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Housing agreements, leases, campus procedures, and local law vary.</em></p>
