        'Getting Involved on Campus' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Campus involvement can mean a club, paid job, research group, service project, student publication, recreation, cultural center, peer program, faith community, or one recurring event. Choose activities that add connection or skill without consuming the time needed for class, work, health, or caregiving.</p>

<h2>Start from a purpose</h2>
<p>Decide whether you want friendship, professional exploration, service, leadership, creative work, exercise, identity-based community, or campus change. Search the official events calendar, organization directory, departments, student life, career services, recreation, cultural centers, and community-engagement office.</p>

<h2>Sample before committing</h2>
<p>Attend several open meetings or events and speak with current members. Ask what members actually do, how often they meet, when peak periods occur, and whether beginners are supported. Recognition by the institution does not prove a group is safe, active, well managed, or the right fit.</p>

<h2>Calculate time and cost</h2>
<p>Include preparation, transportation, travel, fundraising, equipment, meals, and event hours—not just the meeting. Ask about dues, fee waivers, grants, uniforms, and refunds. Begin with one recurring commitment and add another only after understanding the academic workload.</p>

<h2>Make involvement accessible</h2>
<p>Commuters and working or caregiving students can use daytime events, short-term projects, online participation where offered, or roles tied to class and work hours. Contact accessibility services and the organizer early when an event creates a disability barrier. Ask about physical, communication, dietary, and transportation access.</p>

<h2>Turn attendance into connection</h2>
<p>Show up repeatedly, learn names, volunteer for a bounded task, and follow up with one person. You do not need an officer title for involvement to matter. Keep notes on work produced, skills used, hours, and outcomes so the experience can later support a resume or application.</p>

<h2>Watch for unhealthy organizations</h2>
<p>Leave groups that demand secrecy about harm, humiliation, dangerous consumption, sleep deprivation, financial exploitation, illegal activity, or isolation from other relationships. Hazing is not leadership development or a necessary tradition. Use school safety and hazing-reporting routes when needed.</p>

<h2>Review each term</h2>
<p>Ask whether the activity still supports your goals, respects boundaries, and fits the semester. Step down properly, transfer records, and return funds or property when leaving a role. Sustainable involvement is more valuable than accumulating titles while missing classes or exhausting yourself.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: extracurricular and career preparation</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2025-2026/vol2/ch6-consumer-information-and-school-reporting">Federal Student Aid Handbook: campus safety and hazing information</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws">U.S. Department of Education: civil-rights laws</a></li>
</ul>
