        'What Is Student Government?' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Student government is a student-led representative body, often called SGA, student senate, association, council, or assembly. Its authority varies enormously. It may allocate activity fees, recognize organizations, appoint students to committees, pass resolutions, advocate to administrators, or run programs—but it is not usually the institution’s governing board.</p>

<h2>Find its actual authority</h2>
<p>Read the constitution, bylaws, election code, finance rules, school policy, and organizational chart. Determine which decisions are binding, advisory, delegated, or subject to administrative approval. A resolution can express student opinion without changing institutional policy.</p>

<h2>Understand common roles</h2>
<p>Structures may include a president or executive team, legislative representatives, judicial or elections bodies, class councils, campus or school-based seats, and committees. Some representatives are elected; others are appointed. Advisors support compliance but should not be confused with elected decision makers.</p>

<h2>Know where money comes from</h2>
<p>Student government may recommend or control distributions from mandatory or optional student activity fees within law and school rules. Review public budget documents, funding criteria, conflict rules, reimbursement requirements, audits, and appeal routes. Members should never mix organization funds with personal accounts.</p>

<h2>Participate without running</h2>
<p>Attend meetings open under applicable policy, read agendas and minutes, contact a representative, join a committee, comment on a proposal, or apply for an appointed seat. Ask how accessibility, remote attendance, speaking time, and records requests work. Student government is only one route; administrators and formal complaints may own the issue.</p>

<h2>If you run for office</h2>
<p>Confirm eligibility, filing dates, credit or enrollment requirements, campaign finance, endorsements, posting, digital communication, debates, voting, conduct, and complaint procedures. Separate campaign resources from school or organization resources unless permitted. Plan for the weekly workload and periods of intense public disagreement.</p>

<h2>Use responsible governance</h2>
<p>Disclose conflicts, protect nonpublic student information, document decisions, follow procurement rules, and transfer records to successors. FERPA and other privacy obligations may limit what the institution can share, even with elected students. Do not promise confidentiality you cannot provide.</p>

<h2>Evaluate impact realistically</h2>
<p>Ask whether the body communicates decisions, represents commuters and less-visible populations, follows through, and explains limits. Effective advocacy often requires evidence, coalition building, a specific request, and the correct administrator—not only a vote or social-media post.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/family-educational-rights-and-privacy-act">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2025-2026/vol2/ch6-consumer-information-and-school-reporting">Federal Student Aid Handbook: student and institutional disclosures</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws">U.S. Department of Education: civil-rights laws</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. The institution’s constitution, bylaws, election code, fee rules, and delegated authority control.</em></p>
