        'Greek Life Explained' => <<<'HTML'
<p>“Greek life” commonly refers to fraternities and sororities, including social, cultural, service, professional, religious, and honor organizations that use Greek-letter names or similar chapter structures. Organizations differ in purpose, membership, housing, governance, and risk. Do not treat them as one experience.</p>

<h2>Understand chapter relationships</h2>
<p>A chapter may be recognized by the college, chartered by a national organization, governed by a campus council, housed off campus, or some combination. Verify each status independently. A group can lose school recognition yet continue recruiting, and recognition does not certify that every activity is safe.</p>

<h2>Learn the joining process</h2>
<p>Recruitment may be called rush, intake, recruitment, or membership education. Ask about eligibility, dates, selection, time, academic expectations, confidentiality, and whether accepting an invitation creates a financial commitment. Membership practices and lawful criteria vary by organization and type.</p>

<h2>Calculate full cost and time</h2>
<p>Request written dues, initiation, housing, meals, insurance, apparel, event, travel, and fine information, plus payment plans and refund rules. Include meetings, service, education, social events, and leadership work. Do not rely on “typical dues” from another chapter.</p>

<h2>Review housing and conduct</h2>
<p>Chapter housing may use a separate lease or occupancy agreement that school withdrawal does not cancel. Read alcohol, guest, event, transportation, academic, social-media, and conduct rules from the school, chapter, council, and national body. Determine which office receives safety or financial concerns.</p>

<h2>Hazing is not a membership requirement</h2>
<p>Hazing can include humiliation, threats, isolation, sleep deprivation, physical risk, coerced labor, dangerous consumption, compelled illegal conduct, or other abuse—whether called tradition, bonding, or voluntary. Leave immediate danger and call 911. Do not assume you must confront the group alone or complete an activity to keep membership.</p>

<h2>Check current safety information</h2>
<p>The Stop Campus Hazing Act amended federal campus-safety law to add hazing-related disclosures and prevention requirements for covered institutions. Read the institution’s current hazing policy, Annual Security Report, prevention information, and campus hazing transparency report when available. Also review chapter and national conduct histories from official sources.</p>

<h2>Choose based on behavior</h2>
<p>Ask how leaders respond to boundaries, disability access, finances, substance-free participation, and reports of harm. Speak with members and people who chose not to join. Belonging should not require secrecy about injury, coercion, discrimination, or illegal conduct. It is valid to decline or leave.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2025-2026/vol2/ch6-consumer-information-and-school-reporting">Federal Student Aid Handbook: Stop Campus Hazing Act and Clery disclosures</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://ope.ed.gov/campussafety/">U.S. Department of Education: campus safety data</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Organization status, law, school policy, costs, housing, and reporting options vary; verify the specific chapter.</em></p>
