        'Balancing School, Work & a Social Life' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Balance is not giving school, work, relationships, and rest equal time every day. It is building a schedule that protects essential responsibilities and can survive a difficult week. Start with fixed commitments, include the hidden hours around them, and limit optional commitments until the workload is real.</p>

<h2>Calculate the whole week</h2>
<p>Map class, commuting, job shifts, caregiving, sleep, meals, healthcare, and recurring personal obligations. Add preparation and travel. Then schedule study blocks based on course demands. The credit count alone does not reveal labs, clinicals, group work, reading, or major project weeks.</p>

<h2>Set three levels of priority</h2>
<p>Identify nonnegotiables, important flexible activities, and optional extras. During exams or work peaks, reduce the optional layer first. Define a minimum healthy routine for sleep, food, medication, movement, and contact with supportive people rather than sacrificing all recovery.</p>

<h2>Coordinate work early</h2>
<p>Give an employer the class schedule and known exam or break dates as early as possible. Request availability changes in writing and propose workable alternatives. Know shift-swap and call-out procedures. Federal Work-Study jobs are intended to be part time, but award amounts do not guarantee a job or eliminate the need to monitor hours.</p>

<h2>Make social time intentional</h2>
<p>Choose plans that fit energy, money, transportation, and the next day’s obligations. A meal, study session, walk, or recurring club can maintain connection without consuming an entire evening. Say no directly and early. Healthy friends do not require missed exams, unsafe travel, overspending, or substance use.</p>

<h2>Use academic flexibility before crisis</h2>
<p>Attend office hours, tutoring, and advising when performance first slips. Ask instructors about available options without assuming an extension. Before dropping a course or reducing credits, check academic progress, financial aid, insurance, housing, athletics, benefits, and immigration consequences.</p>

<h2>Recognize overload</h2>
<p>Repeated missed classes, unsafe fatigue, panic, inability to eat or sleep, frequent illness, or falling behind in every area means the plan needs intervention. Contact advising, counseling, health services, accessibility services, a supervisor, or the dean of students. In immediate danger call 911; for a U.S. mental-health crisis, call or text 988.</p>

<h2>Run a weekly reset</h2>
<p>Review upcoming deadlines, shifts, travel, meals, spending, and one thing to remove or delegate. Protect time for the highest-risk course and for rest. A realistic schedule with empty space is stronger than a perfectly packed calendar that collapses after one delay.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: budgeting and time management</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/8-things-federal-work-study/">Federal Student Aid: Federal Work-Study</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://988lifeline.org/">988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Employment, attendance, aid, and support policies vary; obtain individualized advice before changing enrollment.</em></p>
