        'Tips for First-Generation College Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>“First-generation” usually means a student is among the first in their family to complete a bachelor’s degree, but programs use different definitions. It does not mean you are unprepared or that your family cannot help. It means some college vocabulary, unwritten expectations, and administrative systems may be new—so make those systems visible.</p>

<h2>Ask how your school defines eligibility</h2>
<p>The federal TRIO definition and a campus scholarship, orientation, or support program may not be identical. Ask the program rather than self-disqualifying. TRIO Student Support Services, where funded, can serve eligible first-generation, low-income students, and students with disabilities; students join a local funded project, not a national grant application.</p>

<h2>Build an office map</h2>
<p>Know the difference between an academic advisor, registrar, financial aid, student accounts, dean of students, accessibility services, career services, and counseling. When redirected, ask for the person, form, deadline, and a warm handoff. Save names and written answers.</p>

<h2>Translate college language</h2>
<p>Look up credit hours, prerequisites, office hours, add/drop, withdrawal, satisfactory academic progress, degree audit, and catalog year. Ask what a form changes academically and financially before signing. No one is born knowing this vocabulary, and other students’ confident answers may still be wrong.</p>

<h2>Create a support team</h2>
<p>Use one academic contact, one peer connection, and one practical support office. Consider first-generation programs, mentoring, cultural centers, tutoring, librarians, faculty office hours, career services, and alumni networks. Share information with family in plain language while keeping your own preferences and FERPA privacy choices clear.</p>

<h2>Make the finances visible</h2>
<p>Review the aid offer, student account, indirect costs, loan amounts, work expectations, and renewal requirements separately. Federal Work-Study must be earned through employment. Complete the FAFSA for each year aid is needed and track school, state, and scholarship deadlines. Ask before using private debt or paying an unexpected charge.</p>

<h2>Seek opportunity without guessing</h2>
<p>Professors may expect students to ask about research, recommendations, office hours, and graduate study. Career staff can explain internships and professional norms. Apply even when you have questions, but verify eligibility and deadlines. Keep a resume and record accomplishments from the first year.</p>

<h2>Treat help-seeking as navigation</h2>
<p>If a policy, academic setback, family expectation, or financial problem is difficult, bring the exact notice and ask for next steps. Using support is not evidence that you do not belong; it is how complex institutions are designed to be navigated.</p>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/about/ed-offices/ope/trio">U.S. Department of Education: Federal TRIO Programs</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: first-time student guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/8-things-federal-work-study/">Federal Student Aid: Federal Work-Study</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Program definitions and eligibility differ; ask each campus program directly.</em></p>
