        'Tips for Nontraditional Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>“Nontraditional student” has no single universal definition. It may describe adults returning after time away, student parents, veterans, part-time students, workers, caregivers, independent students, or people changing careers. Start with your actual constraints and goals rather than trying to match a label.</p>

<h2>Choose a workable program structure</h2>
<p>Compare course times, delivery format, sequence, internship or clinical hours, attendance, maximum time to degree, transfer credit, prior-learning options, and how often required courses run. Ask what happens if you attend part time or stop out. Flexible marketing does not guarantee every required course is available evenings or online.</p>

<h2>Map old credit and current requirements</h2>
<p>Send official transcripts and obtain a written evaluation showing how credit applies—not only how many credits transfer. Review catalog year, residency credits, major GPA, recency rules, and licensure requirements. Work experience may strengthen an application but does not automatically produce academic credit.</p>

<h2>Build around work and caregiving</h2>
<p>Create a weekly plan including commuting, childcare, eldercare, employment, study, sleep, and backup coverage. Ask about predictable scheduling, emergency absence rules, dependent-care resources, and campus childcare. At institutions receiving the grant, CCAMPIS supports campus-based childcare services for eligible low-income student parents, but availability is local.</p>

<h2>Review aid as your current self</h2>
<p>Submit the FAFSA and ask how dependency status, enrollment intensity, prior borrowing, annual and lifetime limits, satisfactory academic progress, and employer tuition assistance affect options. Part-time enrollment can change aid and loan status. Compare grants, scholarships, employer benefits, payment plans, and loans after taxes and reimbursement conditions.</p>

<h2>Use adult-serving resources</h2>
<p>Look for an adult learner office, veteran center, transfer center, TRIO or Educational Opportunity Center, career services, tutoring, online library help, and evening advising. Ask for appointments that match work hours. Build peer contact through class, cohort, professional associations, or student-parent groups.</p>

<h2>Request access early</h2>
<p>Contact accessibility services if a disability creates barriers; colleges generally require the student to request adjustments through the postsecondary process. For pregnancy, medical conditions, military obligations, or caregiving emergencies, identify the correct policy and office before assuming an instructor alone can approve a program-level change.</p>

<h2>Use experience without doing everything alone</h2>
<p>Your work and life experience can strengthen planning, communication, and applied learning. It does not remove the need to learn new technology, ask about academic conventions, or seek tutoring. Schedule orientation to the LMS, library, citation rules, and career transition just as deliberately as class.</p>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/about/ed-offices/ope/trio">U.S. Department of Education: TRIO and Educational Opportunity Centers</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/grants-and-programs/grants-special-populations/grants-economically-disadvantaged-students/child-care-access-means-parents-school-program-84335a">U.S. Department of Education: CCAMPIS childcare program</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/">Federal Student Aid</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: disability access</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Program schedules, aid, childcare, transfer, and support availability are institution- and student-specific.</em></p>
