        'First-Year Student Questions Megathread' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Use this megathread for practical questions about the transition into a first year at a U.S. college: schedules, syllabi, housing, commuting, meeting people, campus services, money, and getting help. Advice here is general; your institution’s catalog, calendar, contract, and official office provide the controlling answer.</p>

<h2>Before posting</h2>
<p>Search the thread and the school’s official website, then state what you already checked. Use a descriptive question rather than “help.” Include the type of institution, undergraduate or graduate status, residential or commuter situation, relevant deadline, and the exact policy wording if known.</p>

<h2>Protect your privacy</h2>
<p>Do not post your name, student ID, birth date, address, room number, class schedule, phone number, account screenshot, financial-aid identifiers, immigration document, medical record, login link, or another person’s private information. Redact barcodes, QR codes, signatures, case numbers, and metadata. Contact the official office for account-specific answers.</p>

<h2>Good academic questions</h2>
<ul>
  <li>How should I interpret this syllabus or grading rule?</li>
  <li>Which office handles placement, prerequisites, registration, or a degree audit?</li>
  <li>How can I prepare for office hours, tutoring, or the first exam?</li>
  <li>What should I check before adding, dropping, or withdrawing?</li>
</ul>

<h2>Good practical questions</h2>
<ul>
  <li>How do I compare campus housing, commuting, meal plans, or transportation?</li>
  <li>What should roommates discuss?</li>
  <li>How do student organizations, work-study jobs, or campus events operate?</li>
  <li>Where can first-generation, transfer, international, disabled, parenting, or adult students find support?</li>
</ul>

<h2>Questions that need an official answer</h2>
<p>Only the school can confirm your bill, aid, registration, accommodation, housing assignment, conduct case, immigration record, or appeal deadline. Ask the registrar, financial aid, student accounts, accessibility services, housing, designated school official, or office named in the notice. Do not rely on a reply here to preserve a deadline.</p>

<h2>Safety and urgent support</h2>
<p>Do not wait for a forum response to an immediate threat, medical emergency, suspected overdose, violence, or missing person. Call 911 or campus emergency services. For a U.S. mental-health crisis, call or text 988. Use confidential campus or community support where desired and ask about confidentiality limits.</p>

<h2>How to answer well</h2>
<p>Separate personal experience from policy, identify the institution or state when relevant, link official sources, avoid diagnosing or giving legal conclusions, and explain which office can verify the answer. Do not shame students for unfamiliar vocabulary or different finances, age, family support, disability, housing, or path to college.</p>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: first-time college student guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://988lifeline.org/">988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is a peer discussion space, not an emergency, legal, medical, financial-aid, or official school channel.</em></p>
